<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arjunawiwaha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1030</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arjunawiwaha, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>'s Wedding&quot;, is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> poem (kakawin) written around 1030 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-kanwa">Mpu Kanwa</a> at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. Based on an episode of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, it tells of Arjuna's penance, his resistance to temptation and his victory over a demon king, and is often read as an allegory of Airlangga's own rise. It became a lasting source for Javanese shadow-puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem was the first kakawin of the East Javan period. It is set when the five Pandawa brothers have lost everything to their cousins the Korawa, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>, an excellent archer and the third brother, seeks a weapon from the gods through meditation and asceticism, according to Robson on Mount Indrakila as a symbol of Mount Meru. The demon Niwatakawaca troubles the abode of the gods and can be defeated only by a man. The gods test Arjuna with seven apsaras sent to seduce him, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, disguised as an old Brahmana, questions him on religion.</p><p>Having passed both tests, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> defeats Niwatakawaca and is rewarded with seven days in heaven and marriage to each of the seven apsaras in turn. In one strand of the story he shoots a boar that a hunter also claims, until he realises the hunter is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, who gives him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupata-shaivism">Pashupata</a> weapon. Its moral is to renounce one's own liberation from the world and stay in it for the welfare of others. At the end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-kanwa">Mpu Kanwa</a> says he is about to follow King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> into battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> literature is rare in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and the Arjunawiwaha was reworked in later centuries: Susuhunan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a> wrote it in macapat verse as Serat Wiwaha Jarwa or Mintaraga, dated 1778, and a version attributed to Yasadipura I but written by Carel Frederik Winter, Sr. was published in 1868. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, where it was counted with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, Bharatayuddha, Bhomantaka and Sutasoma among the important texts, and its hymn to Siwa is recited in temple ritual. Reliefs of the story were carved at Candi Kedaton, Candi Surawana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candi-jago">Candi Jago</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> and at Rambut Siwi in Bali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The first known translation, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, was made by R. Ng. Poerbatjaraka in 1926 and is thought to have a few gaps. Henry published a partial translation in 1981, Wiryamartana an Indonesian translation in 1990 with a long study of how the text changed over the centuries, based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, and Robson a complete translation in 2008. Robson had written in 1972 on how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> texts were still read and studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arjunawiwaha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
